 more objectively)<br>- Decision support: drafting narratives, PRDs/OKRs, r=
isk registers, board memos - so teams spend time on judgment, not formattin=
g<br><br>Where AI cannot replace leadership<br>- Making the bet: what you w=
ill not build, what you=E2=80=99ll double down on, what you=E2=80=99ll walk=
 away from<br>- Setting decision rights: who owns the call when data confli=
cts, and what =E2=80=9Cgood=E2=80=9D looks like<br>- Governance + trust: qu=
ality, compliance, auditability, accountability - especially in regulated i=
ndustries<br><br>A practical way I think about =E2=80=9CAI strategy=E2=80=
=9D is three loops:<br>- Sense (AI-heavy): detect real signals, not noise<b=
r>- Decide (human-owned): make tradeoffs + commit<br>- Deliver (system-owne=
d): measure outcomes, kill fast, scale what works<br><br>The companies winn=
ing with AI won=E2=80=99t be the ones with the most tools. They=E2=80=99ll =
