ox">=E2=80=9CAI will replace developers.=E2=80=9D

From what I see operating a real product business: AI isn=E2=80=99t replaci=
ng engineers - it=E2=80=99s changing what =E2=80=9Cgreat=E2=80=9D looks lik=
e.

Code is getting cheaper. Ownership and judgment are getting more valuable.
AI can generate drafts, refactors, tests, docs - fast.

But in production, the hard part is still very human:
=E2=9C=85 defining the right problem
=E2=9C=85 making the right tradeoffs
=E2=9C=85 validating edge cases
=E2=9C=85 building secure, maintainable systems
=E2=9C=85 communicating clearly across product, ops, and stakeholders

The best engineers I work with don=E2=80=99t =E2=80=9Cfight=E2=80=9D AI. Th=
ey use it like a strong copilot - and then apply what machines still can=E2=
=80=99t: context, taste, accountability.

My take: AI won=E2=80=99t eliminate developers.
It will eliminate the version of the job that was mostly typing.

Question for the community: what skill will matter most for engineers in 20=
26=E2=80=932027 - fundamentals, system design, or communication?
